1Stanford University
2Independent Researcher
Abstract
LLM-based auto-annotators have become a key component of the LLM de-
velopment process due to their cost-effectiveness and scalability compared
to human-based evaluation. ||| However, these auto-annotators can introduce
biases that are hard to remove. ||| While these approaches show a high correlation with human
annotators, they often do so by exploiting spurious correlations such as the length of the
output, the presence of lists, or various position biases Li et al. (2023); Zheng et al. (2023);
Koo et al. (2023); Wang et al. (2023); Wu & Aji (2023)."
91a46d3587e840fdb1b4a46d42ffca7c,bf2d676b63dd4264aedba2488dfbab48,gu2025surveyjudge5,c_r1pift,ai,True,False,True,gu2025surveyjudge,gu2025surveyjudge,True,Supported,Supported,True,high,0.99,background,background,True,False,False,False,False,3,4,1.0,1.0,Prior work has documented both the usefulness and the biases of LLM evaluators.,Prior work has documented both the usefulness and the biases of LLM evaluators.,True,Prior work has documented both the usefulness and the biases of LLM evaluators.,,"Large Language Models (LLMs) have achieved remarkable success across diverse domains, leading to the emergence of ""LLM-as-a-Judge,"" where LLMs are employed as evaluators for complex tasks. ||| A Survey on LLM-as-a-Judge 4.2 Bias As LLM-as-a-Judge becomes more widely deployed, it has been observed to manifest a range of salient biases, even in cases where its evaluations align with human judgments. ||| Such internal biases may propagate into the LLM-as-a-Judge setting, potentially resulting in unfair evaluation outcomes and, in turn, influencing the development of downstream LLMs.","Large Language Models (LLMs) have
achieved remarkable success across diverse domains, leading to the emergence of ""LLM-as-a-Judge,"" where
LLMs are employed as evaluators for complex tasks. ||| A Survey on LLM-as-a-Judge
4.2
Bias
As LLM-as-a-Judge becomes more widely deployed, it has been observed to manifest a range of
salient biases, even in cases where its evaluations align with human judgments. ||| Such internal biases may propagate into the LLM-as-a-Judge setting, potentially
resulting in unfair evaluation outcomes and, in turn, influencing the development of downstream
LLMs. ||| This section systematically reviews these domains, outlining how LLM-as-a-Judge
contributes to reliable and scalable evaluation in each.
5.1
Machine Learning
Large Language Models (LLMs) have emerged as powerful evaluative tools across a wide range
of machine learning (ML) tasks, giving rise to the paradigm of “LLM-as-a-Judge”."
