Abstract
Although measuring held-out accuracy has
been the primary approach to evaluate general-
ization, it often overestimates the performance
of NLP models, while alternative approaches
for evaluating models either focus on individ-
ual tasks or on speciﬁc behaviors. ||| While performance on held-out data is a useful
indicator, held-out datasets are often not compre-
hensive, and contain the same biases as the training
data (Rajpurkar et al., 2018), such that real-world
performance may be overestimated (Patel et al.,
2008; Recht et al., 2019). ||| Further, by summarizing
the performance as a single aggregate statistic, it
becomes diﬃcult to ﬁgure out where the model is
failing, and how to ﬁx it (Wu et al., 2019). ||| In particular, “behavioral testing” (also
known as black-box testing) is concerned with test-
ing diﬀerent capabilities of a system by validating
the input-output behavior, without any knowledge
of the internal structure (Beizer, 1995)."
91a46d3587e840fdb1b4a46d42ffca7c,bf2d676b63dd4264aedba2488dfbab48,shinn2023reflexion8,c_6j7z0q,ai,True,False,True,shinn2023reflexion,shinn2023reflexion,True,Supported,Supported,True,high,0.96,background,background,True,False,False,False,False,3,6,1.0,1.0,"Self-Refine iteratively revises outputs using self-generated feedback, while Reflexion uses verbal feedback and memory to improve subsequent attempts; later analyses identify conditions under which intrinsic or externally supported correction helps or harms.","Self-Refine iteratively revises outputs using self-generated feedback, while Reflexion uses verbal feedback and memory to improve subsequent attempts; later analyses identify conditions under which intrinsic or externally supported correction helps or harms.",True,"Self-Refine iteratively revises outputs using self-generated feedback, while Reflexion uses verbal feedback and memory to improve subsequent attempts.",,"Self-Refine is effective but is limited to single-generation reasoning tasks. ||| Self-reflection The Self-Reflection model instantiated as an LLM, plays a crucial role in the Reflexion framework by generating verbal self-reflections to provide valuable feedback for future trials. ||| These self-evaluation and self-improvement steps are conditioned on given task constraints, such as ""How can this generation be written in a more positive way"".","These self-evaluation
and self-improvement steps are conditioned on given task constraints, such as ""How can this genera-
tion be written in a more positive way"". ||| Self-Refine is effective but is limited to single-generation
reasoning tasks. ||| Concretely, Reflexion agents verbally reflect
on task feedback signals, then maintain their own reflective text in an episodic
memory buffer to induce better decision-making in subsequent trials. ||| Reflexion converts binary or scalar feedback from the
environment into verbal feedback in the form of a textual summary, which is then added as additional
context for the LLM agent in the next episode. ||| Self-reflection
The Self-Reflection model instantiated as an LLM, plays a crucial role in the
Reflexion framework by generating verbal self-reflections to provide valuable feedback for future
trials. ||| In the RL setup, the trajectory history serves as the short-term
memory while outputs from the Self-Reflection model are stored in long-term memory."
91a46d3587e840fdb1b4a46d42ffca7c,bf2d676b63dd4264aedba2488dfbab48,snell2025scaling15,c_thhewt,ai,True,False,True,snell2025scaling,snell2025scaling,True,Supported,Supported,True,high,0.97,predictive,predictive,True,False,False,False,False,2,5,1.0,1.0,"Chain-of-thought and self-consistency alter inference procedures, while adaptive-compute methods allocate additional inference based on difficulty or estimated benefit.","Chain-of-thought and self-consistency alter inference procedures, while adaptive-compute methods allocate additional inference based on difficulty or estimated benefit.",True,Adaptive-compute methods allocate additional inference based on difficulty or estimated benefit.,,"In order to be feasible in practice, a compute-optimal scaling strategy conditioned on difficulty needs to first assess difficulty and then utilize the right scaling strategy to solve this problem. ||| The compute-optimal strategy is defined as a function of the difficulty of this prompt.","Estimating Question Difficulty for Compute-Optimal Scaling
In order to effectively analyze the test-time scaling properties of the different mechanisms discussed in
Section 2 (e.g. the proposal distribution and the verifier), we will prescribe an approximation to this
optimal strategy 𝜃∗
𝑞,𝑦∗(𝑞)(𝑁) as a function of a statistic of a given prompt. ||| The compute-optimal strategy is defined as a function of the difficulty of
this prompt. ||| Concretely, we select
the best performing test-time compute strategy for each difficulty bin independently. ||| In order to be feasible in practice,
a compute-optimal scaling strategy conditioned on difficulty needs to first assess difficulty and then
utilize the right scaling strategy to solve this problem. ||| Given the above re-
sults, it is clear that question difficulty can be a
useful statistic to predict the optimal search strat-
egy to use at a given compute budget."
91a46d3587e840fdb1b4a46d42ffca7c,bf2d676b63dd4264aedba2488dfbab48,wang2023selfconsistency14,c_thhewt,ai,True,False,True,wang2023selfconsistency,wang2023selfconsistency,True,Supported,Partially Supported,False,high,0.9,causal,causal,True,False,False,False,False,3,6,1.0,1.0,"Chain-of-thought and self-consistency alter inference procedures, while adaptive-compute methods allocate additional inference based on difficulty or estimated benefit.","Chain-of-thought and self-consistency alter inference procedures, while adaptive-compute methods allocate additional inference based on difficulty or estimated benefit.",,Chain-of-thought and self-consistency alter inference procedures.,,"In this paper, we introduce a novel decoding strategy called self-consistency to replace the greedy decoding strategy used in chain-of-thought prompting (Wei et al., 2022), that further improves language models’ reasoning performance by a significant margin. ||| Finally, we aggregate the answers by marginalizing out the sampled reasoning paths and choosing the answer that is the most consistent among the generated answers. ||| In this paper, we propose a new decoding strategy, self-consistency, to replace the naive greedy decoding used in chain-of-thought prompting.","In this paper, we introduce a novel decoding strategy called self-consistency to replace the greedy
decoding strategy used in chain-of-thought prompting (Wei et al., 2022), that further improves
language models’ reasoning performance by a signiﬁcant margin. ||| In this paper, we propose
a new decoding strategy, self-consistency, to replace the naive greedy decoding
used in chain-of-thought prompting. ||| In an effort
to address this shortcoming, Wei et al. (2022) have proposed chain-of-thought prompting, where
a language model is prompted to generate a series of short sentences that mimic the reasoning
process a person might employ in solving a task. ||| First, a language
model is prompted with a set of manually written chain-of-thought exemplars (Wei et al., 2022). ||| Next, Published as a conference paper at ICLR 2023
we sample a set of candidate outputs from the language model’s decoder, generating a diverse set of
candidate reasoning paths. ||| Finally, we aggregate the answers by marginalizing out the sampled reasoning paths and
choosing the answer that is the most consistent among the generated answers."
91a46d3587e840fdb1b4a46d42ffca7c,bf2d676b63dd4264aedba2488dfbab48,wang2024notfair2,c_r1pift,ai,True,False,True,wang2024notfair,wang2024notfair,True,Supported,Supported,True,high,0.97,background,background,True,False,False,False,False,3,3,1.0,1.0,Prior work has documented both the usefulness and the biases of LLM evaluators.,Prior work has documented both the usefulness and the biases of LLM evaluators.,True,Prior work has documented both the usefulness and the biases of LLM evaluators.,,"Specifically, we demonstrate that GPT-4 exhibits a preference for the first displayed candidate response by consistently assigning it higher scores, even when the order of candidates is subtly altered. ||| It prompts GPT-4 to score and compare candidate responses and provide explanations, making it a valuable tool for evaluation. ||| In summary, our key contributions are: 1) We reveal that LLMs exhibit severe positional bias, compromising their fairness as evaluators; 2) We develop a calibration framework with three simple yet effective strategies to calibrate the positional bias of LLMs; 3) We manually annotate the “win/tie/lose” outcomes of responses from ChatGPT and Vicuna-13B in the Vicuna benchmark and demonstrate the effectiveness of our proposed approach through experimental results, which show closer alignment with human judgments. 2 Positional Bias of the LLM Evaluator 2.1 LLMs as Evaluators Recently, a lot of works (Zheng et al., 2023; Wang et al., 2023a; Sun et al., 2023; Zhou et al., 2023) have been utilizing LLMs such as GPT-4 as evaluators to compare the performance of two AI assistants.","It
prompts GPT-4 to score and compare candidate
responses and provide explanations, making it a
valuable tool for evaluation. ||| Specifically, we demonstrate that
GPT-4 exhibits a preference for the first displayed
candidate response by consistently assigning it
higher scores, even when the order of candidates
is subtly altered. ||| In summary, our key contributions are: 1) We re-
veal that LLMs exhibit severe positional bias, com-
promising their fairness as evaluators; 2) We de-
velop a calibration framework with three simple yet
effective strategies to calibrate the positional bias of
LLMs; 3) We manually annotate the “win/tie/lose”
outcomes of responses from ChatGPT and Vicuna-
13B in the Vicuna benchmark and demonstrate the
effectiveness of our proposed approach through ex-
perimental results, which show closer alignment
with human judgments.
2
Positional Bias of the LLM Evaluator
2.1
LLMs as Evaluators
Recently, a lot of works (Zheng et al., 2023; Wang
et al., 2023a; Sun et al., 2023; Zhou et al., 2023)
have been utilizing LLMs such as GPT-4 as evalu-
ators to compare the performance of two AI as-
sistants."
