a4bcdb84de344556b9b29545fb3b4608,6bcc9f6f32df4b59a427e4cd83381f61,rajpurkar2016squad0,c_1fl16p8,ai,True,False,True,rajpurkar2016squad,rajpurkar2016squad,True,Supported,Supported,True,high,0.99,other,other,True,False,False,False,False,2,3,1.0,1.0,The field of question answering has been dominated by supervised methods for competitive tasks such as the Stanford question answering dataset (SQuAD).,The field of question answering has been dominated by supervised methods for competitive tasks such as the Stanford question answering dataset (SQuAD).,True,The cited work introduces the Stanford Question Answering Dataset (SQuAD).,,"To address the need for a large and high-quality reading comprehension dataset, we present the Stan- ford Question Answering Dataset v1.0 (SQuAD), freely available at https://stanford-qa.com, consisting of questions posed by crowdworkers on a set of Wikipedia articles, where the answer to every question is a segment of text, or span, from the corresponding reading passage. ||| We present the Stanford Question Answering Dataset (SQuAD), a new reading comprehension dataset consisting of 100,000+ questions posed by crowdworkers on a set of Wikipedia articles, where the answer to each question is a segment of text from the corresponding reading passage. We analyze the dataset to understand the types of reasoning required to answer the questions, leaning heavily on dependency and constituency trees. We build a strong logistic regression model, which achieves an F1 score of 51.0%, a significant improvement over a simple baseline (20%). However, human performance (86.8%) is much higher, indicating that the dataset presents a good challenge problem for future research. The dataset is freely available at https://stanford-qa.com.","To address the need for a large and high-quality
